  "text": "To find the area of the heptagon \\(AFNBCEM\\), we need to break it down into smaller parts and use the information given about the triangle and the quadrilateral \\(DEGF\\).\n\n### Step-by-Step Reasoning\n\n1. **Determine the lengths of \\(AD\\) and \\(EB\\):**\n   Given \\(AD = 4\\) and \\(EB = 8\\), we can find the length of \\(AB\\):\n   \\[\n   AB = AD + EB = 4 + 8 = 12\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the lengths of \\(AF\\) and \\(GC\\):**\n   Given \\(AF = 13\\) and \\(GC = 26\\), we can find the length of \\(AC\\):\n   \\[\n   AC = AF + GC = 13 + 26 = 39\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the lengths of \\(DE\\) and \\(FG\\):**\n   Given \\(DE = 16\\) and \\(FG = 52\\), we can find the length of \\(DF\\):\n   \\[\n   DF = DE + FG = 16 + 52 = 68\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the area of the triangle \\(ABC\\):**\n   Since the area of quadrilateral \\(DEGF\\) is 288, we need to find the area of triangle \\(ABC\\). The quadrilateral \\(DEGF\\) is part of the triangle \\(ABC\\), and the remaining part of the triangle is \\(BDF\\) and \\(CDE\\).\n\n5. **Determine the area of triangle \\(BDF\\):**\n   Since \\(D\\) and \\(F\\) are reflections, \\(DF\\) is the same as \\(DE + FG = 68\\). The area of triangle \\(BDF\\) can be found using the ratio of the sides:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } \\triangle BDF = \\frac{DE \\cdot FG}{DE + FG} = \\frac{16 \\cdot 52}{68} = \\frac{832}{68} = 12.2353 \\approx 12\n   \\]\n\n6. **Determine the area of triangle \\(CDE\\):**\n   Similarly, the area of triangle \\(CDE\\) can be found using the ratio of the sides:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } \\triangle CDE = \\frac{DE \\cdot FG}{DE + FG} = \\frac{16 \\cdot 52}{68} = \\frac{832}{68} = 12.2353 \\approx 12\n   \\]\n\n7. **Determine the area of triangle \\(ABC\\):**\n   The area of triangle \\(ABC\\) is the sum of the areas of triangles \\(BDF\\), \\(CDE\\), and quadrilateral \\(DEGF\\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } \\triangle ABC = \\text{Area of } \\triangle BDF + \\text{Area of } \\triangle CDE + \\text{Area of } DEGF = 12 + 12 + 288 = 312\n   \\]\n\n8. **Determine the area of the heptagon \\(AFNBCEM\\):**\n   The heptagon \\(AFNBCEM\\) is the union of the areas of triangles \\(AFN\\), \\(BNC\\), \\(CEM\\), and \\(EMD\\). Since \\(D\\) and \\(G\\) are reflections, the areas of \\(EMD\\) and \\(EMG\\) are equal. The area of the heptagon can be found by subtracting the areas of triangles \\(AFN\\), \\(BNC\\), and \\(CEM\\) from the area of triangle \\(ABC\\).\n\n9. **Determine the area of the heptagon:**\n   The area of the heptagon is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of heptagon } AFNBCEM = \\text{Area of } \\triangle ABC - \\text{Area of } \\triangle AFN - \\text{Area of } \\triangle BNC - \\text{Area of } \\triangle CEM = 312 - 12 - 12 - 12 = 288\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the heptagon \\(AFNBCEM\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{312}\n\\]",
